Key mechanics relevant to status handling: `responseWriter.WriteHeader` only records `w.status` (it never touches the real `http.ResponseWriter` and does not mark the response as written); the real header is emitted only by `WriteHeaderNow` (which sets `size = 0`, making `Written()` true). `serveError` (gin.go:650) relies on this: it pre-sets `writermem.status = code`, runs the no-route/no-method chain, and only emits the default 404/405 body when nothing was written and the status is still `code`; otherwise it calls `WriteHeaderNow()` to flush whatever the handlers set. Commands used: `grep -n ...`, `awk 'NR>=A && NR<=B {printf "%d\t%s\n", NR, $0}'`. No verification tests were run since the task was read-only inspection; no files were modified.

# Answer

**The LAST element wins.** In `/gin/binding/form_mapping.go`, `setFormMap` writes `ptrMap[k] = v[len(v)-1]` (line 399, with the literal comment `// pick last`). For a form key with multiple values, the destination `map[string]string` gets the final element of the `[]string`. No error is returned. (Contrast: when the destination is `map[string][]string`, the whole slice is copied and all values are kept.)

Verified empirically (repo copied to /tmp, /gin untouched):
```
map[string]string: err=<nil> map=map[k:last]        // input {"k": {"first","middle","last"}}
map[string][]string: err=<nil> map=map[k:[first middle last]]
```

